    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d362">

                <pb facs="1059" n="1849" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v01p2/pg1849"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.03/2–1054: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >confidential<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-02-10T12:00:00+01:00">February 10,
                        1954—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Dulte 61. For Acting Secretary and Phleger from
                    Secretary. Re Tedul 35.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d362-f1"/> Believe proposed amendment is something Executive could
                    live with without serious embarrassment although doubtless Supreme Court would
                    be very curious as to real purpose and its final verdict could not confidently
                    be taken for granted. I feel that if this amendment could be avoided, this
                    should be done to prevent many years of legal controversy and uncertainty. On
                    the other hand, if political considerations seem to call for some affirmative
                    action on the subject of executive agreements, this is probably innocuous as
                    could be devised. However, I doubt President could affirmatively <pb facs="1060"
                        n="1850" xml:id="pg1850"/> support amendment before Congress and country for
                    if our interpretation is correct, effect too slight to justify the dignity of
                    constitutional amendment.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
